# Backdoor Memory Lock API — Quick Start

Lets any client (CPU, GPU0, GPU1, …) read/write a range of the backdoor mapfile
**without colliding** with another client's overlapping range.

## The one rule

> **Lock the range → read/write it → release it.**

Everyone uses the *same three calls* — no CPU/GPU roles.

## The calls

| Call | Use it to… |
|------|------------|
| `BackdoorMemLockInit(path)` | Set up, once per process (same `path` as the store). |
| `CheckAndWaitBeforeReadWrite(addr, size, mode, timeout_ms)` | **Wait + lock** a range before touching it. |
| `ReleaseLockForMemory(addr, size)` | **Unlock** when done. |

`mode` is `BDMEM_READ` (shared — many readers OK) or `BDMEM_WRITE` (exclusive).
`timeout_ms = 0` means wait forever. Returns `BDMEM_OK` on success.

Helpers: `LockForMemory()` (block forever, no timeout), `TryLockForMemory()`
(non‑blocking, returns `BDMEM_BUSY` if taken).

## What you get

- Overlapping ranges (any write) → **serialized** (no clobber / no half‑read).
- Disjoint ranges → **run in parallel** (no false blocking).
- Many readers share; a writer is exclusive.

## Simplest example

```c
#include "backdoormem_coherence.h"

BackdoorMemLockInit("/path/to/store");          // once
unsigned char* p = BackdoorGetAddr_c("main", PADDR, "region", 0);

/* writing */
if (CheckAndWaitBeforeReadWrite(PADDR, SIZE, BDMEM_WRITE, 5000) == BDMEM_OK) {
    /* ... write p[0..SIZE) via the fast pointer ... */
    ReleaseLockForMemory(PADDR, SIZE);
}

/* reading */
if (CheckAndWaitBeforeReadWrite(PADDR, SIZE, BDMEM_READ, 5000) == BDMEM_OK) {
    /* ... read p[0..SIZE) — sees a complete, consistent snapshot ... */
    ReleaseLockForMemory(PADDR, SIZE);
}
```

## C++: can't‑forget‑to‑unlock version

```cpp
{
    BackdoorMemRange lock(PADDR, SIZE, BDMEM_WRITE);   // locks here
    if (lock.ok()) {
        /* ... write the range ... */
    }
}   // auto-released here (even on early return or exception)
```

## Good habits

- Lock the **whole range** you'll touch, in the right **mode**; always release.
- Lock per **region/batch** (lock once, do many accesses) — not per byte.
- Only protects clients that actually call the lock (it's advisory/cooperative).

## Same host vs. cross host — *no API change*

Identical code both ways. `BackdoorMemLockInit` auto‑detects the storage:
- **Local file** → fast OFD locks.
- **NFS/cluster file** → POSIX locks + `msync` (slower, page‑granular).

Only extra step for cross‑host: call once after mapping —
`BackdoorMemLockBindMapping(base_va, base_pa, size)` — and keep the store on the
shared mount.

## Return codes

`BDMEM_OK` (0) · `BDMEM_TIMEOUT` (1) · `BDMEM_BUSY` (2, TryLock only) · `BDMEM_ERR` (-1)

## Files

- `backdoormem_coherence.h` — the API + C++ RAII guard
- `backdoormem_coherence.cpp` — implementation (same‑host + cross‑host)
- `backdoormem_coherence_example.c` — usage patterns
- `backdoormem_coherence_test.cpp` — multi‑process/‑thread test harness
